  "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $-1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $-1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.\n$$",
